#VAR(Table, TABLE)
#ADDTBL("../encoding.tbl", Table)
#ACTIVETBL(Table)
#VAR(PTR, CUSTOMPOINTER)
#CREATEPTR(PTR, "LINEAR", 0, 16)
#JMP($0000127C)

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C06)
I glanced at the Minimum Attendance Chart.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C2E)
In the end, I hadn’t managed to catch the new Blood Tune episode%N
live. No big deal, though—someone had 
<DICT>
upped
</DICT>
 it to MewTube after%N
the fact, so I’d been able to just watch it there.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C46)
I still didn’t feel any better, though.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C56)
Those last words Shogun had left behind...
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C66)
It was almost as if he knew about “God’s gaze” and how much it%N
terrified me. It was way too specific for it to just be a%N
coincidence.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C76)
Maybe someone had leaked some info about me online. Maybe it was%N
another ESO player with a grudge against Neidhardt or something.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C86)
Prodigies like me got a lot of love, so naturally, they also got a%N
lot of hate.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000C96)
Well, most haters out there were just jealous. You wouldn’t see
themaround much, but every once in a while, some child would try to%N
start shit because he was jealous of Neidhardt’s strength and%N
popularity.%K They weren’t worth anyone’s time.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000CA6)
But we’ve gone off topic now.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000CB6)
Anyway, yeah, that Shogun guy really got to me. Earlier I’d tried%N
logging on to ESO to grind for a little while, but I just couldn’t%N
concentrate.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000CC6)
And to make matters worse, of all days for this to happen...
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000D32)
【拓巳】
“It just *had* to be a shitty day like this...”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E18)
On top of everything else that’d happened, I also had to go to%N
school today.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E28)
I seriously didn’t want to go, but if I didn’t follow the%N
Minimum Attendance Chart to a T, my calculations would get%N
completely thrown off.%N
Worst-case scenario, I’d even have to repeat the year.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E38)
【拓巳】
“But I’m so tired...”
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000E48)
I stifled a yawn and started trudging off toward school, a%N
trip that was about ten minutes from my base on foot.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000EB0)
As soon as I started walking, my mood sank even lower.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000EC0)
My body grew heavy, and I found it strangely hard to breathe,%N
causing me to break into an uncomfortable sweat. This was what%N
always happened on school days.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000ED0)
Going to school was pure suffering.%N
Why did I have to go through this...?%N
Grumbling to myself with my eyes fixed on the pavement,%N
I kept on walking.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F42)
I turned to enter Shoto Park—the school was just past it.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F52)
Over by the lake, an elderly couple was taking their morning%N
stroll. Nearby, a businessman sat on a bench, reading the%N
sports section of the newspaper.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F62)
For a park on the outskirts of a bustling city like Shibuya,%N
it was surprisingly calm.
<LINE>
Definitely a great place to relax.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F72)
I took a seat on another bench nearby.%N
Before heading into school in the morning,%N
I always came here to eat breakfast.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F82)
I gazed out toward the peaceful pond, the water surrounded by trees%N
with off-color leaves, their hues slowly changing as autumn rolled in.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000F92)
As per usual, today’s menu was just a cream bun that I’d picked%N
up from the convenience store on the way here. It’d be enough to%N
tide me over until later tonight.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000FA2)
I always skipped lunch.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000FB2)
Nobody wanted to eat lunch with an otaku freak like me, and there%N
was no way in hell I’d eat alone when surrounded by people eating%N
and chatting together.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $00000FC2)
After washing down my breakfast with some Cola,%N
I began staring up at the sky absentmindedly.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000010AC)
No matter the season,%N
the sky above Shibuya was always pretty bleak.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000010BC)
My parents only lived a few train stations away,%N
but there was still a clear difference between here and there.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000010CC)
Don’t get me wrong, I didn’t especially *mind* the bleak sky...%N
but I also couldn’t really explain why.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000011CE)
I glanced at my watch to check the time.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000011DE)
I needed to get going or I’d be late.%N
I had to avoid that outcome at all costs.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000011EE)
I only came to school two to three times a week,%N
so I had to keep a low profile.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $000011FE)
To make it through school,%N
I had to blend in—to become invisible like the air.
<END>

#WRITE(PTR, $0000120E)
The ideal school day was one where nothing happened—one where I%N
didn’t have to deal with any of my teachers or DQN classmates.%N
That was the ultimate goal I shot for, and it would continue to%N
be until the day I’d finally graduate.
<END>
